WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:05.630
Once upon a time there was no sun, no solar
system, no us.

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:11.070
Just a cloud of gas and dust, a solar
nebula.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:19.260
We are here today because billions of years
ago there was a nebula containing all the

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:21.000
necessary ingredients.

00:00:22.890 --> 00:00:28.380
Everything that we see in our solar system
today that was all part originally of the

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:29.820
cloud of gas and dust.

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:31.650
That was our solar nebula.

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:39.360
Almost 5 billion years ago, a solar nebula
was prepared to give birth to our sun.

00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:45.660
So we have billions of years ago our solar
nebula cloud of gas and dust.

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:48.150
And it's hanging out, but it's unstable.

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:53.430
What tips the balance to turn a cloud of gas
into solid objects.

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:57.030
Something has to change inside a nebula.

00:00:57.030 --> 00:01:00.150
Something has to trigger the formation of
stars and planets.

00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:01.800
And that remains a mystery.

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:06.120
So what's the answer to this 5 billion year
old mystery?

00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:15.230
There are two theories.

00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:19.170
Both start with fossils.

00:01:20.310 --> 00:01:25.140
Fossils that make their way from the edge of
the solar system towards Earth.

00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:28.030
Break through our atmosphere.

00:01:29.010 --> 00:01:30.810
And find their way to us.

00:01:35.170 --> 00:01:40.000
Meteorites are really important for us to
understand and study because they're time

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:43.930
capsules to when the solar system was
basically first forming.

00:01:46.290 --> 00:01:51.240
Meena Wadhwa curates one of the largest
collections of meteorites on the planet.

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:57.960
These rocks hold a pristine record of the
very early history of the solar system.

00:01:58.830 --> 00:02:04.470
There was nothing else around in the solar
system before these rocks were formed.

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:05.580
There was no earth.

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:06.710
There were no other planets.

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:08.010
It's mind blowing.

00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:14.670
The first solid objects form out of a cloud
of dust surrounding our newborn star.

00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:20.970
Asteroids and meteorites forming at the same
time contain the chemical fingerprint of our

00:02:20.970 --> 00:02:21.960
solar nebula.

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:28.890
They actually contain some of the oldest
materials oldest solids that condensed from

00:02:28.890 --> 00:02:32.100
the cloud of gas and dust as our solar
system was forming.

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:36.780
And so they came together and formed this
this big rock that you see here.

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:39.060
In 2017.

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:45.180
Researchers analyzing the composition of a
type of rocky meteorite called Chondrites

00:02:45.210 --> 00:02:48.420
find a clue about how our solar system was
formed.

00:02:49.770 --> 00:02:54.570
There might be, in fact, a smoking gun
somewhere in the chemistry of these rocks

00:02:54.570 --> 00:02:59.040
that could tell us about what exactly
happened and how our solar system was formed.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:04.590
This smoking gun is a radioactive element
called Iron 60.

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:08.120
And it's thought to be created only in
supernovas.

00:03:12.610 --> 00:03:17.860
If you have a nebula which is about ready to
start forming stars and a supernova goes off

00:03:17.860 --> 00:03:22.840
next to it, that supernova is going to dump
all those heavy elements into that gas cloud.

00:03:23.350 --> 00:03:27.640
But it's also going to trigger the formation
of stars by slamming into that gas and

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:28.450
compressing it.

00:03:31.150 --> 00:03:37.270
A nearby star goes supernova, the shockwave
strikes our solar nebula, injecting it with

00:03:37.270 --> 00:03:38.320
Iron 60.

00:03:42.580 --> 00:03:47.620
But the collision starts a runaway
gravitational collapse in the core of the

00:03:47.620 --> 00:03:54.250
nebula. The gas cloud clumps together
becoming hot and dense.

00:03:54.550 --> 00:03:56.260
Our sun is born.

00:03:58.180 --> 00:04:03.340
As the sun is forming, there's basically a
cloud of junk all around the sun.

00:04:03.340 --> 00:04:08.230
And as it orbits the sun, it kind of
accretes or sticks together and grows into

00:04:08.230 --> 00:04:09.190
these balls.

00:04:11.320 --> 00:04:17.230
Over the next hundred million years, these
balls get bigger and bigger, forming

00:04:17.230 --> 00:04:20.200
asteroids, moons and planets.

00:04:22.520 --> 00:04:25.740
All of the planets in our solar system seem
very different.

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:28.520
Some ice giants, some gas giants, some rocky
bodies.

00:04:29.970 --> 00:04:34.890
But in fact, all of these planets came from
the same Presolar Nebula.

00:04:36.140 --> 00:04:43.100
And on one small planet, the right cocktail
of elements gave rise to us.

00:04:44.810 --> 00:04:50.330
Everything, every atom in our bodies was once
part of the Presolar Nebula.

00:04:52.790 --> 00:04:58.550
The theory that a supernova nudged our solar
system into existence is compelling.

00:04:59.330 --> 00:05:01.040
But not everyone agrees.

00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:06.650
Sometimes the biggest arguments among
scientists are caused by the littlest things.

00:05:06.650 --> 00:05:10.340
And in this case, I'm talking about little,
tiny radioactive atoms.

00:05:12.530 --> 00:05:20.330
In 2017, studies reveal other meteorites
contain a different radioactive signature a

00:05:20.330 --> 00:05:23.930
rare isotope of aluminum called aluminum.

00:05:23.930 --> 00:05:25.040
26.

00:05:26.190 --> 00:05:30.210
That's a rather odd atom that is not formed
very easily in supernovas.

00:05:30.210 --> 00:05:32.550
So that had to come from somewhere else.

00:05:34.230 --> 00:05:41.850
That somewhere else is a rare type of giant
star, 40 to 50 times the mass of our sun.

00:05:42.330 --> 00:05:44.670
A wolf rayet star.

00:05:46.380 --> 00:05:51.570
Stars can be very weird, and the very massive
stars are incredibly weird.

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:56.430
The largest type of star that we've seen is
what's known as a Wolf-Rayet star.

00:05:58.820 --> 00:06:04.010
Wolf Rayet stars burn the hottest of all
stars, producing heavy elements like aluminum

00:06:04.010 --> 00:06:06.440
26 during their short lives.

00:06:07.310 --> 00:06:12.650
These are massive and hot and luminous, and
they blow off a tremendous wind.

00:06:13.470 --> 00:06:20.700
This stellar wind ejects tons of matter from
the star into the surrounding space, creating

00:06:20.700 --> 00:06:22.050
a bubble structure.

00:06:23.830 --> 00:06:29.620
Scientists see this process at work in the
bubble nebula 7000 light years from Earth.

00:06:31.540 --> 00:06:36.820
In the middle of the nebula is one of these
giant stars with a massive stellar wind.

00:06:36.850 --> 00:06:38.920
High energy particles, radiation.

00:06:38.920 --> 00:06:43.060
And just like the name suggests, it's
blowing a bubble in the larger nebula around

00:06:43.060 --> 00:06:43.420
it.

00:06:45.150 --> 00:06:49.530
The walls or shell of the bubble are dense
and full of matter.

00:06:49.560 --> 00:06:58.260
The stellar wind pushes more and more matter
into the shell until this material collapses

00:06:58.290 --> 00:07:01.470
under its own gravity and condenses into
stars.

00:07:03.880 --> 00:07:08.890
It's entirely possible that what we're seeing
in the bubble Nebula is what happened here

00:07:08.890 --> 00:07:13.360
four and a half or more billion years ago to
form the sun and the planets.

00:07:14.440 --> 00:07:20.110
If our solar system formed within a
Wolf-Rayet bubble nebula, it would explain

00:07:20.110 --> 00:07:23.380
why so much aluminum 26 is present in
meteorites.

00:07:27.190 --> 00:07:28.990
But the jury is still out.

00:07:31.410 --> 00:07:36.780
What we do know is that our story began with
the collapse of the solar nebula.

00:07:38.580 --> 00:07:41.610
But one day our star will die.

00:07:43.070 --> 00:07:49.610
Will the sun turn into a stunning nebula, or
will it just fade to black?

